WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.935
-Our next guest is a very funny
actor, comedian, and writer

00:00:02.935 --> 00:00:05.070
whose work you've seen on
"Detroiters," "Drunk History,"

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:06.239
and right here on "Late Night."

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.740
And because she's a writer here,

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:10.944
she wrote this next part
and insisted I read it.

00:00:10.944 --> 00:00:14.246
And now, the most beautiful
comedian and writer

00:00:14.246 --> 00:00:19.252
I've ever seen, a real live
princess, Amber Ruffin.

00:00:19.252 --> 00:00:20.986
-Seth?

00:00:20.986 --> 00:00:22.488
-Oh, hi, Amber.

00:00:22.488 --> 00:00:24.156
-Seth Meyers?

00:00:24.156 --> 00:00:25.591
-Yes, yes, Amber.

00:00:25.591 --> 00:00:28.694
-Well, I wasn't expecting a call
from you.

00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:30.363
I would have dressed up.

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:31.998
-Oh, Amber.

00:00:31.998 --> 00:00:34.867
So that's just your lying around
the house outfit?

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:38.238
-Yes, this is my lounge gown.

00:00:38.238 --> 00:00:39.805
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:44.710
Now, Amber, you've been on
the show many times on camera

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.211
over the course of
our 1,000 shows.

00:00:46.211 --> 00:00:49.149
This is the first time
I've interviewed you, though.

00:00:49.149 --> 00:00:50.649
Are you excited for this moment?

00:00:50.649 --> 00:00:54.387
-Yes, it's everything I've ever
dreamed of and more.

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:56.622
You in your weird attic.

00:00:56.622 --> 00:00:58.424
Me in my hallway.

00:00:58.424 --> 00:00:59.825
-[ Laughs ]

00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:02.428
And you know who forced my hand
was Anderson Cooper

00:01:02.428 --> 00:01:04.263
because you were on Anderson
Cooper's show last night.

00:01:04.263 --> 00:01:06.332
And you were wonderful,
and he's wonderful.

00:01:06.332 --> 00:01:07.934
And I was thinking, "Well, now,

00:01:07.934 --> 00:01:09.768
now I feel like I have to
interview you."

00:01:09.768 --> 00:01:11.771
-Yeah, he appreciates me, Seth.

00:01:11.771 --> 00:01:13.305
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:13.305 --> 00:01:14.607
I do -- I would like to --

00:01:14.607 --> 00:01:17.810
I would like to show a clip of
the end of the interview

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:21.581
where you didn't tell Anderson
you loved him.

00:01:21.581 --> 00:01:23.716
You said you "loved him, too."
-Yeah.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.019
-And it really made
Anderson Cooper laugh.

00:01:26.019 --> 00:01:28.387
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:28.387 --> 00:01:31.624
-Well, Amber Ruffin, I'm glad --
I'm so glad you were on tonight

00:01:31.624 --> 00:01:33.225
'cause I've been watching
what you've been doing

00:01:33.225 --> 00:01:35.294
and I just think it's really
powerful and important.

00:01:35.294 --> 00:01:36.462
So thank you.

00:01:36.462 --> 00:01:37.997
-I love you, too, Anderson.

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.799
-[ Laughs ]

00:01:39.799 --> 00:01:43.102
-Amber, we've known each other
for a long time.

00:01:43.102 --> 00:01:45.137
We met, because we both worked
for a theater

00:01:45.137 --> 00:01:47.706
in Amsterdam called
Boom Chicago.

00:01:47.706 --> 00:01:50.142
You were one of the first people
we hired for this show.

00:01:50.142 --> 00:01:53.613
But one of the things we found,
I think, having you on camera

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:56.249
and the way you write, is that
you can both talk about

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:59.385
serious things and connect with
people in a way

00:01:59.385 --> 00:02:02.521
that also brings them joy.

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:04.824
Did you -- When you first
started doing stuff on camera,

00:02:04.824 --> 00:02:06.925
is that something
you knew about yourself?

00:02:06.925 --> 00:02:12.465
-No. I thought I was just
going to take this job,

00:02:12.465 --> 00:02:17.503
keep my head down,
and not get fired.

00:02:17.503 --> 00:02:21.474
Then I found out that you guys
really let people

00:02:21.474 --> 00:02:25.812
do whatever they want,
so I got to acting up.

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:28.014
-Well, you know,
we've been really lucky

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:31.918
because we have a writing staff
that, you know, has --

00:02:31.918 --> 00:02:33.886
is diverse enough to talk about
a lot of different things.

00:02:33.886 --> 00:02:35.054
And things have happened,

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:36.623
particularly the events of
this last month

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.558
where, you know, we called you
up and said,

00:02:39.558 --> 00:02:41.560
"Hey, do you want to have
something ready for Monday?"

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:44.797
And it was your suggestion
to come on and tell

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.865
your real-life experiences
with police.

00:02:46.865 --> 00:02:49.135
And initially, you were going to
do it on Monday,

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:51.104
and then you also offered
that you could tell

00:02:51.104 --> 00:02:53.005
a different story every day.

00:02:53.005 --> 00:02:54.206
What made you think that that

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:56.308
would be the most powerful way
to address it?

00:02:56.308 --> 00:02:59.311
-You know, when it started,
I started writing, you know,

00:02:59.311 --> 00:03:05.317
bits about the protest, and then
that felt weird and dumb.

00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:12.191
But it just became such
an opportunity, you know?

00:03:12.191 --> 00:03:15.194
I was like, "Oh, we write jokes
about stuff all the time,

00:03:15.194 --> 00:03:16.362
but this is an opportunity

00:03:16.362 --> 00:03:20.066
to really let people
know some [bleep]."

00:03:20.066 --> 00:03:21.133
Some stuff.

00:03:21.133 --> 00:03:24.137
[Bleep]

00:03:24.137 --> 00:03:29.909
So I thought, "Oh, let me just
tell a real story,

00:03:29.909 --> 00:03:35.548
recount an actual story,"
and then you guys said, "Great.

00:03:35.548 --> 00:03:37.683
We'll put it
at the top of the show."

00:03:37.683 --> 00:03:41.921
And then the movement
really took off.

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.956
And I was like, "Oh, you know,

00:03:43.956 --> 00:03:46.192
every day still
feels like yesterday."

00:03:46.192 --> 00:03:47.926
You know what I mean?
-Yes.

00:03:47.926 --> 00:03:52.064
-It felt that exact same way
for a really long time.

00:03:52.064 --> 00:03:57.336
And so I thought, "Well, we --
I've got stories for days.

00:03:57.336 --> 00:03:59.638
-And we should point out
that you did four,

00:03:59.638 --> 00:04:01.540
and you're not out.

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:04.009
-I'm not out of police stories.

00:04:04.009 --> 00:04:06.045
They don't like me.

00:04:06.045 --> 00:04:07.813
-And hearing it from someone
like you,

00:04:07.813 --> 00:04:09.882
especially someone who has
established yourself

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:12.618
as a person, who when people see
you, they think,

00:04:12.618 --> 00:04:15.154
"Well, no one would ever have
a problem with Amber."

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:20.459
-Yeah, people think when police
violence happens, they think,

00:04:20.459 --> 00:04:22.060
"Oh, well, what did
that person say?

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:23.329
What did that person do?"

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:25.931
And that gave me a real
superpower here because,

00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.801
you know, if you watch the show,
maybe you've seen me enough

00:04:28.801 --> 00:04:31.570
to come to the conclusion
that I'm a regular dude.

00:04:31.570 --> 00:04:34.106
I'm not running around yelling
at people, demanding [bleep].

00:04:34.106 --> 00:04:37.342
I'm just hanging out
and giggling, and that happened.

00:04:37.342 --> 00:04:39.511
You know?
I mean, can you imagine

00:04:39.511 --> 00:04:44.116
if I wanted them to recognize
an ounce of my rights?

00:04:44.116 --> 00:04:46.752
You know, which I would have
been able to do.

00:04:46.752 --> 00:04:47.953
But I didn't say [bleep].

00:04:47.953 --> 00:04:50.723
I complied completely
and that's what happened.

00:04:50.723 --> 00:04:52.625
Man, it's crazy out there, Seth.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.194
-It's crazy.

00:04:55.194 --> 00:05:00.499
It was awesome to watch them
and we were so lucky to have you

00:05:00.499 --> 00:05:02.201
tell us -- I don't know,
share that with us.

00:05:02.201 --> 00:05:03.202
And I want to thank you.

